        "committer": {
          "name": "Artem Blagodarenko",
          "email": "ablagodarenko@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-11-06 14:48:33.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "subject": "LU-20908 lfs: empty OSTs with lfs migrate --from-ost",
        "message": "LU-20908 lfs: empty OSTs with lfs migrate --from-ost\n\nEmptying an OST with lfs migrate rewrites every object of each file\nthat has one there, one file at a time.\n\nAdd \"lfs migrate --from-ost OST_LIST FILE|FID ...\", which moves only\nthe objects of the given files that are on a listed OST, with\nllapi_ost_object_migrate(), from a pool of worker threads, and leaves\nthe other objects alone.  The MDS chooses the new OSTs, and is kept\noff the OSTs being emptied with max_create_count=0; -i names the new\nOST instead.  Files come from the command line or from --files-from\nor -0, as lfs find writes them: on a client with --ost, or on the OSS\nwith --target, which reads the OST itself.\n\nA file that is open for writing or changes during the copy is set\naside and tried again once the other files have been, and not sooner\nthan --retry-delay seconds; --block moves such files under a group\nlock.  -W limits the bandwidth over all threads.  A journal of\nfinished FIDs lets --resume skip them, and --json writes one result\nper file.  The options of plain lfs migrate keep their meaning where\nthey apply: -i, -b, -W, -0, --files-from, --lustre-dir and\n--stats-interval.\n\nAdd sanity-pfl tests 30a-30i: plain, PFL, overstriped, sparse and\nhard-linked files from lfs find on a client and on the OSS, FLR files,\nFID lists with journal and dry run, -i, files held open for writing,\ndeleted files, and the bandwidth limit.\n\nTest-Parameters: testlist=sanity-pfl\nSigned-off-by: Artem Blagodarenko <ablagodarenko@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5\nChange-Id: Iddd443a6d9d2d3400710c983dd16d7328b6a9c30\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
